digest: e3c0737cbb0fc8bbb4fcff520c05241e248b7035fb40ae9b604f208a10e2503c
  when: 2026-09-22T19:18:59Z
   why: Add two documentation verbs to ctl.sh: docs, a gate, and docs-serve, a viewer. ctl.sh is governed because it carries the conformance verb that decides whether the rules run at all; that verb is UNCHANGED, no rule is disabled, no detector is touched, and conformance passes 6/6 enforced before and after. What cmd_docs adds is a check that every documentation section names the code it describes and still matches it, run inside cmd_lint beside the hook and AGENTS.md assertions. It is on --strict, so a section that claims code and names none fails too; that half is what stops the tree drifting back to unanchored one section at a time while the gate reports green. Falsified both ways before wiring, each time against a real violation with the rest of the tree as the control: moving anchored code flagged exactly the one section describing it and no other, and an unanchored section was caught by name. ONE THING HERE IS A WIDENING AND IT SHOULD BE READ AS ONE. docs-serve --lan publishes the documentation viewer on this machine's LAN address, which is an exception to ARCH-DEV-1 as worded -- developer surfaces bind only to loopback. Loopback remains the default and nothing leaves the machine without --lan, verified in both directions: with the flag the viewer answers on loopback and on the LAN name and refuses on a docker bridge, without it the LAN name refuses. It binds the machine's own default-route address rather than 0.0.0.0 precisely so it is not also served on the forty container bridges and the VPN interface this host carries. ARCH-DEV-1's detectors watch the Go config loader, the GDB listener, the compose file and the committed example environment file, so none of them can see this and the rule passes honestly rather than by accident -- which is the point worth recording: the invariant's WORDS now have an exception its CORPUS cannot observe. That gap belongs in the module decisions record under Deployment and access, either as a recorded exception or by scoping the decision to the emulator's own surfaces; it is not written there yet, and this row is the pointer to it. What it exposes is the repository's own documentation -- not secret, but a detailed description of the box.

